---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="sempods roadmap — spec, retrieval and open tooling"
  description="What exists in sempods today, what is opening next, and which work matters for the decentralized semantic data pod specification."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">status</p>
  <h1>Roadmap without theatre</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    sempods is early. The useful thing is not a list of dates; it is a clear
    distinction between what runs today, what is being opened next, and what the
    model needs in order to become broadly useful.
  </p>

  <div class="status-list">
    <section>
      <h2>Running today</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>public pods serving real event data as JSON-LD</li>
        <li>
          the <a href="https://spec.sempods.org">specification</a>, in its own
          repository and rendered with search and a{' '}
          <a href="https://spec.sempods.org/api/">try-it client</a> over the
          OpenAPI descriptions
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://schema.sempods.org">schema.sempods.org</a>, answering
          with Turtle or a readable page depending on what asked
        </li>
        <li>Kotlin reference implementation</li>
        <li>Linked Data CRUD over HTTP</li>
        <li>SPARQL endpoint with server-enforced context sandboxing</li>
        <li>OAuth-based authorization with context-scoped grants</li>
        <li>pod MCP endpoint at <code>/_system/mcp</code></li>
        <li><a href="https://apps.sempods.org">preview apps</a> that connect to pods by URL</li>
        <li>hosted test pods available on request</li>
        <li>JVM artifacts on Maven Central</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Opening next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>a public TypeScript SDK</li>
        <li>search and optional vector retrieval adapters behind <code>find</code></li>
        <li>simpler examples that do not require starting with the server internals</li>
        <li>better try-it-out paths from the website</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2>What the specification is not yet</h2>

  <p>
    It exists, and it is worth reading, but it does not yet bind anyone. Until{' '}
    <code>0.1</code> is tagged the text is <em>descriptive</em>: it is being
    extracted from the reference implementation, and where the two disagree the
    implementation is right. That is also the window in which a requirement can
    still be renumbered or deleted outright, which stops afterwards and never
    resumes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The distinction matters more than it sounds. A specification that claimed to
    be normative on the day it was published would be asking for trust it has not
    earned from a single independent implementation.
  </p>

  <h2>Model work</h2>

  <p>
    The concrete technical work now is graph retrieval: <code>find</code> as the
    semantic entry point, structural traversal through Linked Data, and MCP tools
    that expose the same pattern to agents.
  </p>

  <p>
    SHACL shapes and a TBox model are still design work, not final
    specification. They may become the place where apps describe expected shapes
    and shared semantics, but the public contract should not pretend that is
    settled.
  </p>

  <p>
    Permission work is in the same category: contexts are the working boundary
    today, while alignment with ACP-style and finer-grained authorization remains
    under active design.
  </p>

  <h2>Agent layer</h2>

  <p>
    AI agents are clients of the pod model. The direction here is graph
    retrieval, MCP, local or personal memory contexts, and provider-independent
    reasoning over the relevant slice of knowledge.
  </p>

  <p>
    The principle should stay simple: the agent sees what its token allows. No
    special permission model, no hidden path around the pod.
  </p>

  <h2>Ecosystem</h2>

  <p>
    The long-term goal is not one hosted sempods platform. It is an open,
    decentralized and federated knowledge web where multiple implementations and
    products can interoperate.
  </p>

  <p>
    If independent teams adopt the same open model for their own
    knowledge-management problems, the personal data vision becomes more
    realistic, not less.
  </p>

  <p class="action-links">
    <a href="/start">Try what runs today</a>
    <a href="/concepts/graph-retrieval">Read graph retrieval</a>
    <a href="https://spec.sempods.org">Read the specification</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-kotlin">Read sempods-kotlin</a>
  </p>
</Base>
